<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiaozhi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Giao Chỉ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiaozhi was the Chinese name for the region of the Red River delta in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. It was a commandery under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> after 111 BCE and, much later, a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> from 1407, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongle-emperor">Yongle Emperor</a> annexed Đại Việt, until 1427, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>'s uprising forced the Ming to withdraw.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiaozhi.jpg" alt="Jiaozhi" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Albert Herrmann &amp; Georg Westermann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mainland_Southeast_Asia_in_1415.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jiaozhi named a historical region corresponding to present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, its commandery centred on the Red River Delta. Chinese chroniclers offered folk etymologies for the name: the scholar Zheng Xuan read it as feet turning in towards each other, the Book of Later Han linked it to men and women bathing in the same river, and the Tongdian to crossed big toes. Pronounced Kuchi in Malay, the name became the Cochin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> of Portuguese traders around 1516.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Much later sources name Giao Chỉ as a district of the native state of Văn Lang, which fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-duong-vuong">Thục Phán</a> around 258 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-tuo">Zhao Tuo</a> took Thục Phán's citadel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/co-loa-citadel">Co Loa</a> around 208 BCE, declared the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyue">Nanyue</a> in 204 and organised its Vietnamese lands as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> of Jiaozhi and Jiuzhen. When the Han conquered Nanyue in 111 BCE and divided it into nine commanderies, Jiaozhi became the centre of their administration, and the whole area was sometimes called by its name.</p><p>Under the Han the capital of Jiaozhi was first Mê Linh and then Luy Lâu. In 39 CE the sisters Trưng Trắc and Trưng Nhị, daughters of the Lac lord of Mê Linh, led an uprising that forced the Grand Administrator Su Ding to flee; Ma Yuan's expedition crushed it and destroyed the natives' bronze drums, and Chinese settlement and intermarriage followed. In 2 CE Jiaozhi reported four times as many households as Nanhai, and by the end of the 2nd century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was its most common religion.</p><p>In the Three Kingdoms period Shi Xie administered it from Longbian on behalf of Wu, and it later adhered to the southern dynasties based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a> before passing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang. In the 670s it was absorbed into the Tang's Annan Protectorate, and the name Annan largely replaced it. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a> drove out the Ming, he divided the country into five dao, and Giao Chỉ was never again the name of an official unit.</p><p>The region also lay on routes to the West. The Book of Liang records that a merchant of Da Qin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, named Qin Lun reached Jiaozhi in 226 and was sent on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a>. In 1877 Ferdinand von Richthofen proposed that Ptolemy's port of Kattigara lay near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, a view widely accepted until archaeology at Óc Eo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> suggested that site instead.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jiaozhi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Funan's sailors tied the Roman world to China: Óc Eo may be Ptolemy's Kattigara, and the first Roman embassy recorded in China arrived in 166 CE through <b>Jiaozhi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funan">Funan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1407 the armies of the Ming Yongle Emperor overthrew the short-lived Hồ dynasty and annexed Đại Việt as the province of <b>Jiaozhi</b>, imposing heavy taxes and labour levies and carrying books and craftsmen off to China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Some were Buddhists: Kang Senghui, a monk of Sogdian family born in <b>Jiaozhi</b> in present-day Vietnam, translated Buddhist texts in southern China in the 3rd century, and the largest body of surviving Sogdian literature is Buddhist, much of it translated from Chinese.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Ming forces captured Hồ Quý Ly, founder of the Hồ dynasty, and his son Hồ Hán Thương in present-day Hà Tĩnh Province and brought them as prisoners to the Yongle Emperor in Nanjing. Đại Ngu became the Ming province of <b>Jiaozhi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
